---
import { sitePath } from '../data/site';
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
---
<Guide title="Replay a secure session" description="Recover the original application requests, then send them through a fresh, authenticated connection." sections={[{id:'tls',label:'TLS and key logs'},{id:'identity',label:'Verify the target'},{id:'ftps',label:'FTPS'},{id:'ssh',label:'SSH'},{id:'limits',label:'Recovery limits'}]}>
  <div class="notice"><strong>Keep recovery material local.</strong>Matching TLS key logs can reveal captured plaintext. Treat them as credentials. This website never accepts captures, keys, or passwords.</div>
  <h2 id="tls">TLS: recover, then reconnect</h2><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Record matching recovery material.</strong>Configure the original client or server to write an NSS-style TLS key log when recording the capture.</li><li><strong>Choose the recorded session.</strong>Inspect the capture and select one supported TLS session. Pass the matching key log explicitly.</li><li><strong>Open a new verified connection.</strong>Livewire recovers requests from the recording, identifies supported inner protocols, and sends them over fresh TLS.</li></ol><Code code={"livewire live tls.pcap -keylog sslkeys.log -t device.example:443"} label="Fresh TLS application replay" /><p>Old TLS ciphertext is not a request template for a new connection. Without matching decryption material, a ciphertext-only capture cannot reveal the original requests.</p>
  <h2 id="identity">Verify the live target</h2><p>Certificate verification is on by default. Use your lab CA and the expected server name when a device has a private certificate or you connect by IP.</p><Code code={"livewire live tls.pcap -keylog sslkeys.log -t 192.0.2.50:443 -ca device-ca.pem -server-name device.example"} label="Private CA · explicit verified identity" /><p>Resolve certificate errors by checking the hostname, CA, certificate validity, and device clock. Do not make verification bypass the default fix.</p>
  <h2 id="ftps">FTPS includes the data connection</h2><p>FTP and explicit or implicit FTPS use a coordinated control/data workflow. Secure replay needs the matching key log and recoverable transfer data. Supported protection transitions and transfer evidence are checked; missing data or unsupported security stops the operation.</p><Code code={"livewire live ftps.pcap -keylog sslkeys.log -t ftp.example:21 -ca lab-ca.pem -server-name ftp.example"} label="Explicit FTPS · fresh session" /><p>Keep the recorded control and data streams together. A successful control login alone does not establish that a file transfer completed.</p>
  <h2 id="ssh">SSH needs explicit operations</h2><p>An SSH capture cannot supply plaintext commands. Provide credentials, the expected host key, and the operations you intend to run. Prefer a dedicated lab key.</p><Code code={"livewire live ssh.pcap -t device.example:22 -user lab -key id_ed25519 -host-key device_host_key.pub -cmd \"show version\" -expect \"Version\""} label="Fresh SSH · pinned host key" /><p>Unified SSH replay requires a pinned host key. Reports retain lengths and hashes instead of command text and response bodies.</p>
  <h2 id="limits">Know when recovery must stop</h2><ul><li>Missing or mismatched TLS secrets cannot decrypt the recording.</li><li>Unknown inner protocols and unsupported authentication are not silently downgraded to ordinary TCP.</li><li>Captured SSH ciphertext cannot be converted into commands.</li><li>DNP3 Secure Authentication and MQTT enhanced authentication are outside the supported automatic replay path.</li><li>A fresh connection can produce new IDs, timestamps, and tokens. Only supported adapters and explicit comparison rules account for those changes.</li></ul><p class="reference-link"><a href={sitePath("/reference/reliability/")}>Read the protocol and security limits →</a></p>
</Guide>
